PREFIX rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>
PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
PREFIX owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#>
PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
PREFIX sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#>
PREFIX dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>
PREFIX oq: <https://semantechs.co.uk/ontology-quality/>

# LOG-002: owl:FunctionalProperty violated by data
#
# A functional property is violated when a subject has two values that are
# two *things*, not two ways of writing one thing. The three filters below
# say what "one thing" covers; each replaced a measured defect in the single
# `FILTER(STR(?o1) < STR(?o2))` this check used to carry, which compared
# lexical forms and so counted any two spellings as two values.
#
#   1. sameTerm   -- the trivial self-pair.
#   2. ?o1 = ?o2  -- value equality, wrapped in COALESCE. Two literals of
#      the same value and different lexical form ("80" and "80.0"^^xsd:decimal)
#      are one value. COALESCE is load-bearing: `=` raises a type error on
#      two literals of datatypes the engine cannot compare, and an error in
#      a FILTER drops the row, which would turn a real violation into
#      silence. COALESCE returns the first expression that evaluates without
#      error, so the comparison answers "not known equal" instead of
#      vanishing.
#   3. owl:sameAs -- two names for one individual. Traversed in both
#      directions and transitively, `(owl:sameAs|^owl:sameAs)+`, because
#      owl:sameAs is symmetric and transitive and an ontology may assert it
#      either way round.
#
# The old filter also caused a false *negative*, which is the worse half:
# SPARQL 1.1 17.4.2.5 defines STR() for literals and IRIs, so STR() of a
# blank node is a type error, the FILTER dropped the row, and a functional
# property with two distinct blank-node values was reported as clean. That
# is the same way STY-003 shipped broken: a check that passes every test and
# reports nothing looks exactly like a correct graph.
#
# Measured on nine cases: the old filter was wrong on four of them, three
# false positives and that false negative.
#
# The join is symmetric, so an offending pair matches twice, once in each
# order. There is no portable way to order two arbitrary RDF terms -- STR()
# is exactly the trap above -- so no tie-break is attempted, and the two
# solutions carry identical focus node, path and value and collapse under
# the (check_id, focus_node, path, value) dedup key. `shapes/logical.ttl`'s
# SHACL twin is written the same way for the same reason, and the two must
# keep reporting the same focus node and path to merge with each other.
#
# This file is shared data: the same bytes ship in the VS Code extension's
# `resources/checks-registry/`. Edit both copies or neither -- see
# tests/test_shared_registry_parity.py.
CONSTRUCT {
  _:r a sh:ValidationResult ;
    sh:resultSeverity sh:Violation ;
    sh:focusNode ?s ;
    sh:resultPath ?p ;
    sh:sourceConstraintComponent oq:LOG-002 ;
    sh:resultMessage ?msg .
}
WHERE {
  ?p a owl:FunctionalProperty .
  ?s ?p ?o1 .
  ?s ?p ?o2 .
  FILTER(!sameTerm(?o1, ?o2))
  FILTER(!COALESCE(?o1 = ?o2, false))
  FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?o1 (owl:sameAs|^owl:sameAs)+ ?o2 }
  BIND(CONCAT("Subject ", STR(?s), " has more than one distinct value for functional property ",
              STR(?p), ", violating its functional-property axiom.") AS ?msg)
}
